PCI backend request retirement
==============================

Run: bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/pciback_lifetime_test.sh

The fixture extracts the production request, deferred worker, reference release
and unlend functions. It uses pthread locks and controlled condition variables
for the boundaries where the kernel uses locks and a workqueue. PCI config
access and reset are modeled, not executed against a physical device.

Four scenarios check:

* A queued request pins its original device grant. Unlend followed by a new grant
  with the same instance ID and BDF cannot redirect that request, or send its ACK
  into a later boot.
* A negative reply requires no config access and does not queue an ID lookup.
* Unlend revokes admission under the registry spinlock, then waits outside that
  lock for a slow config access and its reply before resetting the function.
* Direct and deferred config writes preserve the request value; single-function
  retirement releases the same pins as whole-instance retirement.

The existing startup and image-lock tests also check PCI retirement before
publishing a reusable instance state and before freeing Image segments.

Limits
------

These checks do not prove native lockdep behavior, PCI reset/DMA quiescence, or
RISC-V PCI assignment. They do not cover delayed resource-hotplug ACKs, partial
overlay rollback, or requests still in a peer's shared ring at retirement.
Those are separate lifetime gates; a passing fixture is not end-to-end safety.
